{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"FIX PUSHED as Gerrit change 2613 patchset 2 (357182c8dcf0): https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/2613\nLLM-Review +1 (clean PASS, no findings). Verified -1 from ONE job only -- scanner-integration, the\nknown repo-wide blocker owned by 90c7. Everything else green: lint + type-check, the full pytest\nmatrix (ubuntu py3.11/3.12/3.13, macos py3.13), optionality, artifact-probe, eval-discipline,\ngolden-path, verify-identity, pip-audit.\n\nTHE FIX: the pre-lock 'if _satisfied(...): return' is deleted; readiness, the npm lookup and both\nsteps now live inside 'with _install_lock(js_dir):'.\n\nA SUBTLETY WORTH RECORDING, because it would have been a quiet regression: the npm lookup had to move\nINTO the lock as well, positioned BELOW the readiness check. The old fast path returned before ever\nlooking npm up, so leaving the lookup above the check would newly raise 'npm not on PATH' on an\nalready-provisioned tree that no longer has npm -- a case that previously worked. Covered by a new\ntest (a satisfied tree needs no npm on PATH), and a second mutation hoisting the lookup back above\nthe check kills that test.\n\nTHE REPRODUCTION IS DETERMINISTIC, which was the criterion that mattered most here. Not N workers and\nhope: a child process takes the flock and WHILE HOLDING IT creates precisely what the unlocked check\ninspected -- node_modules/, node_modules/playwright/, and a half-written dist/roundtrip.mjs (esbuild\nwrites in place, so the path exists long before the bytes do) -- appends 'peer-mid-build', sleeps,\nappends 'peer-done', releases. The test blocks until 'peer-mid-build' is on disk, which is a\nPRECONDITION rather than a race, then calls provision_toolchain and appends 'caller-returned'. The\nappend-only log is the oracle and the verdict is an ORDER, decidable in a single run.\n\nRED against the unfixed code:\n  AssertionError: the caller returned while a peer still held the lock over a half-written bundle --\n  a readiness check ran outside the lock: ['peer-mid-build', 'caller-returned', 'peer-done']\nDefect-seeded mutation (restoring the pre-lock check with the fix in place) reproduces the identical\nfailure; reverted, module green.\n\nCardinality oracle for the uncontended path: cold single-worker 'pytest tests/e2e' over an empty\ntests/e2e/js gives 32 passed, and an npm shim on PATH logged EXACTLY TWO invocations -- 'ci' then\n'run build'. So the fix does not double-provision.\n\nAlso confirms the a1f1 retraction from the other side: check_mechanism_delta --check reports new=2\nwith BOTH entries 'admitted' and exit 0, and test_mechanism_delta.py passes 31/31. Marked mechanisms\nwork.\n\nNot closing: 2613 cannot reach Verified +1 until the scanner blocker clears."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788512913113483001,"uuid":"b64be5da-35d8-4bd7-9677-2bbe2ef3a1b8"}